<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/661">661</a>–714</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf was an Umayyad general and governor, the most powerful servant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> Abd al-Malik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a>. He besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 692, ending the caliphate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Ibn al-Zubayr</a>, and then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the east from 694 until his death in 714, crushing the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-ashath">Ibn al-Ash'ath</a>. He sponsored the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and reforms of coinage and administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf.jpg" alt="al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_al-Hajjaj_ibn_Yusuf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Provincial government was entrusted to powerful governors, such as <b>al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</b>, who ruled Iraq and the east from 694 to 714 with notorious severity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After attacks on Arab ships near Debal, the Umayyad governor of Iraq, <b>al-Hajjaj</b>, sent Muhammad ibn Qasim, who took Debal in 711 and defeated and killed Dahir in 712.</blockquote><a href="/state/brahman-dynasty-of-sindh">Brahman dynasty of Sindh</a></li><li><blockquote>In 692 <b>al-Hajjaj</b> besieged and bombarded Mecca; Ibn al-Zubayr died fighting and the Umayyad Caliphate was reunited.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought the Umayyad governors of Sistan for decades and in 698 destroyed the so-called 'Army of Destruction' sent by <b>al-Hajjaj</b>, an event that helped trigger the revolt of Ibn al-Ash'ath.</blockquote><a href="/state/zunbils">Zunbils</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.27,40.42&amp;year=695" aria-label="Ta'if on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5636 1377 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6086 1647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6086 1647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.27,40.42&amp;year=695">What was here in 695 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
